Title: GALACTIC THRONES
Episode: 101, "THE UNREGISTERED FLEET"
Credit: Draft 1 SHORT — PROPOSED, not approved
Author: Prepared for Ω SYD OMEGA 91717 STUDIOS GLOBAL under the founder's direction
Draft date: 2026-11-07
Notes: Cold open and three condensed acts, about 13 script pages of a 50-minute episode. Follows the Season 1 bible; the Founding Compact has ten clauses and this episode breaks clause four. No battle. The 9.1717 Hz frequency is not mentioned. Nothing here is filmed, released or playable.

FADE IN:

INT. THE THRONE HALL OF CONCORD — DAY

A vast white hall with a floor of inlaid stars, one for every world in the Empire. Nine hundred and some nobles stand in rows of exactly equal width. At the far end, a high seat, and on it AETHON IV, sixty, in a robe that looks heavier than he is.

A HERALD reads from a long scroll, not looking at it.

HERALD
"Clause one. That no world shall make war upon another."

Aethon IV lowers his eyes in assent. The rows of nobles murmur the clause back.

HERALD (CONT'D)
"Clause two. That no world shall be taxed without a seat."

MAIN TITLES — TEN STARS IN A RING; NINE SHINE, ONE IS FAINT. THE FAINT ONE IS NUMBERED FOUR.

ACT ONE

INT. THE ADMIRALTY ARCHIVE — LATER

A cold, quiet room of racks. KAELEN VOSS, forties, a strategist's pale uniform with the collar slightly undone, sits with a stack of ledgers. He is the only man in the building who looks relaxed.

ADMIRAL THREX, sixties, fleet-grey, enters with a flask he does not offer.

THREX
You are the only man in the Admiralty who reads the register on jubilee day.

VOSS
The jubilee is the day it is posted.

THREX
It is posted every year. It is correct every year.

VOSS
(turning a page)
Then I will be quick.

THREX
(a beat)
You were a fine gunnery officer.

VOSS
I was a bad one. I counted the shells.

Threx's mouth tightens a degree. He goes.

ACT TWO

INT. THE SPIRE — SERAYA'S STUDY — DAY

A room full of paper. SERAYA, thirties, in an unadorned jacket, walks in with a sheaf. Her desk is a mess of petitions, each pinned with a small wooden marker for the world that sent it.

SERAYA
(to an aide)
Two hundred worlds have signed. Read me the first three.

AIDE
They ask for a seat on the Compact Council. For the right to see the fleet register.

SERAYA
(softly)
That is clause four. They are asking the Empire to obey its own constitution.

AIDE
Your father will say it's rude.

SERAYA
My father will say whatever his Admiral says.

She sets the petition down.

INT. THE ADMIRALTY ARCHIVE — NIGHT

Voss alone. A ledger open. He has been through four hundred ships. He runs a finger down a column, stops, runs back.

VOSS
(quietly)
Four hundred and one.

He opens a second book, the live fleet roll. Four hundred and two.

VOSS (CONT'D)
(to himself)
One extra.

He pulls the register of the orbital yards. A berth has no name beside it. He taps the screen: a single berth, the largest in the yards, with a hull number and no registry entry.

ACT THREE

EXT. ORBITAL SHIPYARD — NIGHT

A cathedral-sized dock, hung in the dark. Voss, in a transit coat, stands on a walkway with a lantern and a pass. Below him, inside a bay hung with scaffolds, a ship: long, matte, unlit.

He holds a tablet up to the hull. The ship's number appears. No entry. He walks closer.

On the hull, small and neat, the Imperial crest; beneath it, in a different hand, a name: "KESTREL." He knows the name. It is the Admiral's.

A voice from a loudspeaker on the dock. Not Threx. Not the Emperor. Calm, female, formal, broadcast across the independent worlds: JUNO ARDENT.

JUNO (V.O.)
(radio, from far away)
To the Empire from the independent worlds. We ask once. Show us the fleet.

Voss listens. The bay lights come on, one at a time, very slowly.

INT. THE ADMIRALTY ARCHIVE — LATER

Voss at his desk. The audit form, blank except for a signature line. He picks up a pen. Sets it down. Picks it up.

He writes his name.

CUT TO:

EXT. ORBIT — THE KESTREL

The ship from the dock, now at the edge of the yards, silent, drifting a degree off the lanes. Its running lights are off. A hatch opens. Inside, a single man in a grey coat watches the yards turn. THREX.

THREX
(very quietly, to nobody)
Then it is begun.

CUT TO BLACK.

TAG

INT. THE THRONE HALL OF CONCORD — NIGHT

The hall is empty. The high seat is empty. On the arm of the seat, a small paper, folded once, left by a servant. Aethon IV's hand, unsigned: "Whose fleet?"

CLOSING SEQUENCE — TEN STARS. NINE SHINE. THE FAINT ONE IS STILL FOUR.

THE END OF EPISODE 101 (DRAFT 1 SHORT)
